WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.460
Good morning, John. I need you to do me a favor:

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:04.000
go and get a coin and flip it ten times.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.460
Actually, everyone who's watching this video right now,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.260
pause it, go get a coin, and flip it ten times.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.480
Now every flip of every coin is going to be entirely random.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.700
But despite that fact, I know what just

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.480
happened when all of you flipped your coins.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.200
I don't know what happened to your coin,

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.340
but I know what happened overall.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.660
And that is because I'm a psychic.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:22.520
Because I am omniscient.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:23.880
Because I have a little camera installed in all of

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.880
your rooms and I WATCH YOU AS YOU WATCH ME.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.120
Okay, not actually, it's because I know about probability.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.040
In fact, as this video will probably get viewed tens of thousands of times,

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.440
I can guarantee you that right now there are a bunch of people who are like

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.740
"Holy crap, man, I just flipped a coin ten times

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.940
and it landed on heads every single time.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.140
I have a magical coin. A magical -

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.420
I should take it to Vegas and bet on it

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.280
because it always lands on heads."

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.380
But guess what, my friend,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:50.480
there's nothing special about your coin

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.340
and there's nothing special about you.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.900
I'm sorry, y- you- you're a beautiful snowflake, you're very special.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.980
Even if the odds of getting ten heads in a row are over one in a thousand

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.080
(which they are, they're 1 in 1024),

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.580
assuming that there are 102,400 people who just flipped their coin ten times,

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:09.980
there are roughly 100 people who just flipped ten heads in a row.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.000
And a hundred people who just flipped ten tails in a row.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.320
This is probability.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.400
It is the science, the act, nay... the ART

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.280
of assigning meaningful numbers to things that no one can actually really know.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.080
Probability lets us make real, predictable and measurable conclusions about random events.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.720
A weird thing about random events is that when they happen once you have no idea what's going to happen.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.660
But oftentimes when they happen many times, patterns are obvious and unavoidable.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.040
So let's do a little terminology now.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.640
Probability is just P.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.340
The event that we're looking for the probability of is expressed by a letter, usually starting with A.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.520
So the probability of A happening is P(A) ["P of A"].

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.040
And if all outcomes are equally likely, P(A) equals

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.120
the number of events where A is true, divided by the total number of events.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.880
So when you all flipped your coins, each individual flip was entirely random.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:01.900
No one, not Miss Cleo, not Albert Einstein, not even Dr. Manhattan

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.000
could know where that coin was going to fall.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.920
But there are two possible outcomes and getting heads is one of those outcomes.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:16.860
So there is a 1 in 2, or one half, or zero-point-five, or fifty-percent chance that it will land on heads.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:22.260
So when we flip a hundred coins roughly fifty of them are going to be heads and fifty are going to be tails.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.560
And we expect some variation around those numbers.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:25.840
Okay, duh, obviously.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.060
But what if we're curious what will happen when we flip two coins?

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.740
Then we're talking about the probability of A and B happening.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:35.960
Very simply, we have a one in two chance twice.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:41.620
And that is 1/2 times 1/2, and assuming you know how to multiply fractions, that is 1/4.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.660
A one-in-four chance that you will get heads and then heads.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.580
But then, if we're doing it ten times, we have 1/2 times itself ten times,

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:52.140
which we can write as (1/2)^10 (one half to the tenth power).

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:56.300
Which my calculator tells me is 1/1024.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.860
This is how I could pretend to be so smart before.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.300
Now let's take things much more complicated, and talk about dice instead of coins.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.480
It's not actually any more complicated.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.540
So if you're rolling a die, the total number of outcomes is six.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.720
And maybe we're concerned about whether or not you're going to get a five.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.000
That's one possible outcome. That's 1/6.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.220
Holy crap, how crazy that you have a one-in-six chance of rolling a five.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.160
But what is the probability of it not being a five?

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.300
That, my friends, is called the opposite, or complement.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.340
And those of you who are actually thinking about that will probably recognize that that is 1 - P(A).

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.560
In the case of you not rolling a five, it is 1 - 1/6, which is 5/6.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.700
So the probability of not rolling a 5 is 5/6.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.980
This doesn't sound complicated, but the idea of 1 - P(A)

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.580
being the opposite or complement is actually very important.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:43.800
But if you want to know the odds of the outcome being an even number then you have to

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.760
take all the even numbers (two, four, and six) and put them over the total number of possible outcomes.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:52.420
And you have 3/6 which is 1/2. So basically, it's the flip of a coin.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.440
So there you have it. The outcomes that you're concerned with over all the possible outcomes.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.340
But sometimes those two numbers are really hard to come by.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:02.300
Like, it's really hard to figure out that the number of possible poker hands is 2,598,960.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.360
Unless you look it up on the internet, which is what I did,

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.800
and that's probably what you should do in that situation, too.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.220
And what about the probability of getting two pair in your poker hand?

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.420
That - how the heck would you figure that out?

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.160
Well, you don't, you look it up on the internet. It's 123,552.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:16.980
So the odds of getting two pair in your poker hand is

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:23.640
123,552 divided by 2,598,960, which is 4.7%. It's so easy!

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:28.740
Because you're all so very smart, you probably have noticed that probabilities fall on a scale of zero to one,

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.720
with zero being an impossible event and one being a certain event.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.480
And this is why probabilities are often represented in percents.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:38.940
I assume that you know this, but percent is just like saying something is out of a hundred.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.740
So when something happens one out of two times,

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.740
it's a probability of 0.5, which is fifty out of a hundred times.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:48.440
So when we talk about rolling a die and figuring out what the probability of it landing on 1 twice is,

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:51.240
we're talking about the probability of A and B happening.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.260
Which is represented like this, and is basically like saying P(A) times P(B).

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.840
Of course, this is only the case when A is not dependent upon what B is.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:04.000
So the probability of rolling snake eyes is 1/6 times 1/6 which is 1/36.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.800
The probability of A or B happening is a completely different thing,

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.460
and that's written like this and is basically P(A) plus P(B).

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:15.120
So the probability of rolling a 2 or a 3 is 1/6 plus 1/6,

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.020
which is 2/6, which is 1/3, which is a one-third probability.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:24.280
So now, because you are theoretically going to have to use this new-found knowledge in actual life somehow,

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:28.180
I'm going to make up some weird-ass situations that have nothing to do with real life

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.540
and use them to test your knowledge of probability.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.420
This is what we call "word problems."

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:35.600
And when I was in school, I hated them and they made me want to kill myself.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.000
But now I'm teaching them so I frakkin' love 'em.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.140
First word problem:

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:43.880
Every time you smoke a cigarette, you have a one-in-five chance of being killed by a terrorist.

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:45.680
And every time you have sex before marriage,

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.500
you have a 40% chance of being devoured by a tank of venomous piranhas.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.840
I'm thinking as long as I make situations up I should make up situations

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.720
that also have value judgments and are at least a little bit interesting.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.940
So if you're not married and you have a cigarette after you have sex, what are the chances that you survive?

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:05.080
I'm going to give you a little probability tip here: always go for "ANDs" and avoid "ORs."

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:09.140
So if you're calculating the chance that we die, it's either the terrorists or the piranhas that are gonna get us,

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:11.440
and that's an OR, and ORs are bad.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:17.380
So make it into an AND, and say in order to survive, we have to both survive the terrorist AND the piranhas.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:20.400
But in order to do that we have to do our 1 - P(A) trick.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.340
So we have our 1 - 1/5, which is 80%, and we have our 1 - 40%, while is 60%,

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.200
and we just multiply those two together and get 48%.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.100
Word problem number 2:

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:34.040
You're at your mother's house and you've been shot by a poison dart by ninjas who've just cut the power.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:35.620
You grope around to find the medicine cabinet but your mother

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.700
always mixes all the pills into one bottle, 'cause she thinks it saves space.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:42.920
Which is dumb because there's a ton of space in the medicine cabinet anyway.

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:46.720
It's dark so you can't see the difference between the pills, and inside the bottle

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.900
there are 6 Tylenols, 4 Imodium ADs and 4 poison dart antidote pills.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.120
You only have time to pick three pills out of the bottle before the poison dart kills you.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:56.740
What are the odds that you survive?

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:58.440
I think all word problems should be life or death.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.000
Okay, so for those of you who thought you were going to

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.100
avoid this problem by just drinking the whole bottle all at once,

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:07.120
you just DIED from poison dart antidote pill overdose.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.660
You're DEAD and YOU CAN'T CHEAT YOUR WAY OUT OF WORD PROBLEMS.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:12.360
You're going to want to start this problem by saying,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.000
"okay, I have a 1/14 chance the first time, and a 1/13 chance the second time,

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.520
and a 1/12 chance the third time," but then you're doing OR again.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.120
It's either this pill OR this pill OR this pill.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.720
What you want is AND. So you have to calculate your chances of not getting an antidote pill.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:32.620
So your chances of not getting an antidote pill are 10/14 times 9/13 times 8/12.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:38.540
You get 33%, and then you just switch back using 1 - P(A) and it's 67% odds of surviving.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:40.100
Final word problem:

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:45.060
You have a gun. There's only one bullet in it, but you don't know which chamber it's in.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.320
You're being attacked by a terrorist velociraptor.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:54.160
If you do manage to shoot the velociraptor, there's only a 30% chance that it will stop before it kills you.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.960
You only have time to pull the trigger four times. What are the chances that you survive?

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.020
Again, we must start by calculating the odds that we do not fire a bullet.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:04.880
Five of six chambers are empty.
*click*

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:06.820
Four of five chambers are empty.
*click*

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:08.960
Three of four chambers are empty.
*click*

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:11.560
Two of three chambers are empty.
*click*

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.000
Multiply all those together to get the odds that you aren't going to shoot a bullet.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.860
That's 32%, and 1 - 32% is 68%, the odds that you will fire a bullet.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:24.680
Then you multiply that by 30% to get the odds that you will actually stop the velociraptor.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:27.340
Your chances of surviving are 20%.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.080
Thank you for watching my video.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:32.920
John, I look forward to watching you educate me more on Wednesday.

